2. The state of being liable for such a payment; the obligation of contributing.

{{smaller block|Undre it there is a Town that hight Sobache; and there alle abowte dwellen Cristene men undre ''Trybute''.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 104.}}}}

{{smaller block|His [Burke's] imperial fancy has laid all nature under ''tribute'', and has collected riches from every scene of the creation and every walk of art.
{{right|''R. Hall'', Apology for the Freedom of the Press, iv.}}}}

3. Formerly, that which was paid by a subject or a tenant to a sovereign or lord; a tax; rental.

{{smaller block|The distinction which we should draw between ''tribute'' and rent was seldom if ever marked in early times. The receiver of ''tribute'' was regarded as the landlord, and he who paid ''tribute'' was regarded as a tenant, paying rent.
{{right|''D. W. Ross'', German Land-Holding, notes, p. 243.}}}}

4. See the quotation.

{{smaller block|"In some of the southern parts of Ireland," said Grattan, in one of the tithe debates, "the peasantry are made tributary to the tithe-farmer, draw home his corn, his hay, and his turf for nothing; give him their labour, their cars, and their horses at certain times of the year for nothing. These oppressions not only exist, but have acquired a formal and distinct appellation—''tributes''."
{{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., xvi.}}}}

5. A contribution; an accretion.

{{smaller block|From his side two rivers flow'd, . . .
Then meeting join'd their ''tribute'' to the sea.
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., iii. 258.}}}}

6. A personal acknowledgment or offering; a mark of devotion, gratitude, or respect.

{{smaller block|He receives a suitable ''tribute'' for his universal benevolence to mankind in the returns of affection and good-will which are paid him by every one that lives within his neighbourhood.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 122.}}}}

{{smaller block|The passing ''tribute'' of a sigh.
{{right|''Gray'', Elegy.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
7. In ''mining'', the proportion of ore or its value which a person doing tribute-work receives for his labor.={{smaller|'''Syn.''' 1. ''Duty'', ''Impost'', etc. See ''tax''.}}

'''tribute''' (trib′ūt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''tributed'', ppr. ''tributing''. [< ME. ''tributen'', < L. ''tributus'', pp. of ''tribuere'', assign, allot, grant, give: see ''tribute'', ''n.''] 1. To pay as tribute.

{{smaller block|An amorous trifler, that spendeth his forenoons on his glass and barber, his afternoons with paint or lust, ''tributing'' most precious moments to the scepter of a fan!
{{right|''Whitlock'', Manners of Eng. People, p. 302. (''Latham.'')}}}}

2†. To distribute; bestow; dispose.

{{smaller block|Hem I sette in wel pastyned lande,
And thai ''tributed'' with felicitee.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 124.}}}}

'''tribute-money''' (trib′ūt-mun″i), ''n.'' Money paid as tribute.

{{smaller block|But Jesus perceived their wickedness, and said, . . . Shew me the ''tribute money''. And they brought unto him a penny.
{{right|Mat. xxii. 19.}}}}

